Titre$ Monografia illustrata del Genere RUSSULA in Europa - Mauro Sarnari – Associazione Micologica Bresadola, Trento, 1998-2005
Com$ Adaptée par Aurélien Paris (13/02/2008)

Key to the Subgenus, Sections, Subsections

1,4$ 1a : Context hard, chalky, browning or blackening, sometimes preliminarily reddening, fruit bodies initially white, then changing like the context, pileus margin smooth, long incurved, lamellulae very abundant, cystidia and basidia slim, pigment vacuolar. $ Subgen. Compactae. $s"Subgen. Compactae."
1,5$ 1b : Taste often acrid or unpleasant, smell usually strong, spore print cream, rarely white, margin of pileus often strongly striate or gelatinous (if margin smooth cf. Consobrinae in 20a), stipe with large cavities, colours dull, reddish-ochre to grey-brown, pigment often intraparietal or incrusting, veil sometimes present. $ Subgen. Ingratula. $s"Subgen. Ingratula."
1,2$ 1c : Colours usually bright, red, green, violet, brown, yellow, etc., lamellulae rare.

2,CS8$ 2a : Dermatocystidia absent everywhere, epicutis hyphal ends big and subulate, pileus and stipe velvety, hymenium with empty pseudocystidia not greying or blackening with SBA. $ Subgen. Amoenula. $s"Subgen. Amoenula."
2,8$ 2b : Pileus green and/or violet, rarely brown, never red, epicutis with dermatocystidia not septate (but they lack in Virescentinae), basidia and cystidia slim, spores usually very small, without an amyloid spot, pigment granular, taste mild or slightly acrid in unripened lamellae. $ Subgen. Heterophyllidia. $s"Subgen. Heterophyllidia."
2,3$ 2c : Not with the above combination of characters.

3,10$ 3a : Epicutis with primordial hyphae (“not incrusted” in Olivaceinae), dermatocystidia absent in the pileus, taste mild. $ Subgen. Incrustatula. $s"Subgen. Incrustatula."
3,13$ 3b : Epicutis with or without incrusted dermatocystidia, rarely including primordial hyphae. Dermatocystidia present at least in stipe cortex if epicutis consisting only of hairs. $ Subgen. Russula. $s"Subgen. Russula."

4,CS1$ 4a : Context blackening, sometimes preliminarily reddening, spores with thin ornamentation, without an amyloid spot, spore print white (at least in European species), pileocystidia usually very small and with diverticulate tips, rarely absent, pigment vacuolar and brownish. $ Sect. Compactae. $s"Sect. Compactae."
4,CS3$ 4b : Habitus lactarioid with a very short stipe, context slightly browning, spore print cream or exceptionally yellow (also white in some exotic species), spores usually with an amyloid spot. $ Sect. Lactarioides. $s"Sect. Lactarioides."
4,CS2$ 4c : Lamellae mostly very distant, habitus hygrophoroid or sect. Compactae-like, spore print white, context more or less browning, spores tiny, lacking an amyloid spot, with very thin ornamentation hardly visible in M.O., cystidia slim and almost cylindrical. $ Sect. Archaeinae. $s"Sect. Lactarioides."

5,CS9$ 5a : Margin of the pileus blunt (habitus like in subgen. Russula), spore print white, spores with amyloid spot, dermatocystidia well characterized, irregularly cylindraceous and septate, incrusted by a thin yellow pigment solidified. $ Sect. Felleinae. $s"Sect. Felleinae."
5,6$ 5b : Margin of the pileus acute to subacute, lamellae sometimes weeping, spore print cream, rarely white, spores usually lacking amyloid spot.

6,0$ 6a : A brownish-yellow arachnoid veil present at least at stipe base, reacting bright red with KOH. - Sect. Subvelatae. $ R. insignis. $s"Sect. Subvelatae."
6,7$ 6b : Veil absent, no reddening reaction with KOH. $ Sect. Ingratae. $s"Sect. Ingratae."

7,CS9$ 7a : Pileus yellow-ochre, spore print white, dermatocystidia very large and spindle-shaped. $ Subsect. Farinipedes. $s"Subsect. Farinipedes."
7,CS9$ 7b : Pileus tawny-ochre to smoky-brown, to ashy-grey, smell generally strong, unpleasant or pleasant, spore print cream, dermatocystidia lanceolate or also spindle-shaped, often with a small rounded capitulum, but small and sometimes tiny. $ Subsect. Foetentinae. $s"Subsect. Foetentinae."

8,0$ 8a : Base of cutis terminal ends and subcutis consisting of spherocysts, dermatocystidia absent on pileus (at least in European species) but present on stipe, pileus surface furfuraceous and areolate. - Sect. Virescentinae. $ R. virescens. $s"Sect. Virescentinae."
8,9$ 8b : Subcutis consisting of thin hyphae, epicutis with dermatocystidia. $ Sect. Heterophyllae. $s"Sect. Heterophyllae."

9,CS4$ 9a : Lamellae fatty or elastic, spore print white, context not turning pink with FeSO4, then slowly greyish green, terminal hyphae and dermatocystidia very thin, the latter not turning blue in SV, hyphae strongly metachromatic with Cresyl Blue. $ Subsect. Cyanoxanthinae. $s"Subsect. Cyanoxanthinae."
9,CS5$ 9b : Lamellae elastic in youth, taste mild, context somewhat browning or yellowing, turning bright-orange with FeSO4, dermatocystidia poorly characterized, not turning blue in SV. $ Subsect. Heterophyllae. $s"Subsect. Heterophyllae."
9,CS6$ 9c : Lamellae fragile, often with a slightly acrid taste, spore print cream, ochre, exceptionally white or light yellow, epicutis consisting of well characterized dermatocystidia, terminal hyphae of the epicutis often formed by short inflated cells. $ Subsect. Griseinae. $s"Subsect. Griseinae."
9,CS7$ 9d : Lamellae more or less fragile, often with a slightly acrid taste, epicutis consisting of hair-like hyphal ends and cylindraceous dermatocystidia, spore print cream to dark ochre. $ Subsect. Ilicinae. $s"Subsect. Ilicinae."

10,11$ 10a : Spore print white or whitish. $ Sect. Lilaceinae. $s"Sect. Lilaceinae."
10,12$ 10b : Spore print yellow, rarely ochre. $ Sect. Amethystinae. $s"Sect. Amethystinae."

11,CS38$ 11a : SV causing a persistent bright reddening in dried fruit bodies, pileus pinkish red or with a shade of orange. $ Subsect. Roseinae. $s"Subsect. Roseinae."
11,CS37$ 11b : SV causing an indistinctive reaction, never bright red in dried specimens, pileus with variable colours. $ Subsect. Lilaceinae. $s"Subsect. Lilaceinae."

12,CS42$ 12a : Usually found under broadleafed trees, fruit bodies sturdy, context thick, becoming purplish with Phenol, primordial hyphae somewhat well-characterized but not incrusted. $ Subsect. Olivaceinae. $s"Subsect. Olivaceinae."
12,CS39$ 12b : Found under conifers, pileus pruinose or somewhat velvety, primordial hyphae wide 6-9(11) µm, cortex of stipe lacking cells turning blue with SBA. $ Subsect. Amethystinae. $s"Subsect. Amethystinae."
12,CS41$ 12c : Fruit bodies mostly medium-sized, context sometimes greying or blackening, hyphal ends more or less cylindric on epicutis, cortex of stipe containing cells blackening with SBA. $ Subsect. Integroidinae. $s"Subsect. Integroidinae."
12,CS40$ 12d : Hyphal ends on epicutis clavate, cortex of stipe containing cells blackening with SBA, cuticle not pruinose or sometimes initially shining. $ Subsect. Chamaeleontinae. $s"Subsect. Chamaeleontinae."

13,14$ 13a : A yellowish veil present at least at the base of the stipe, instantly reddening with KOH.
13,15$ 13b : Veil absent, base of stipe not reddening with KOH.

14,0$ 14a : Fruit bodies very small-sized, with habitus like Tenellae (Rhodellinae), spore print light yellow, taste mild. - Sect. Messapicae. $ R. messapica. $s"Sect. Messapicae."
14,CS25$ 14b : Fruit bodies sturdy, spore print white or cream, taste usually slightly acrid. $ Sect. Viscidinae. $s"Sect. Viscidinae."

15,16$ 15a : Fruit bodies small and slim, context somewhat soft and fragile, turning blue with Gayac, margin of the pileus striated, taste usually mild, dermatocystidia with many septa, not incrusted (a very thin incrustation may occur in some species with a yellow spore print), basidia mostly short. $ Sect. Tenellae. $s"Sect. Tenellae."
15,19$ 15b : Taste usually acrid or very acrid, epicutis containing well characterized dermatocystidia. $ Sect. Russula. $s"Sect. Russula."
15,22$ 15c : Fruit bodies mostly medium, rarely small-sized, context firm, taste often fairly mild, epicutis containing incrusted or smooth dermatocystidia (lacking only in Auratinae), primordial hyphae sometimes present.

16,17$ 16a : Basidia not larger than 50 x 12 µm, context yellowing or almost unchanging, never greying.
16,18$ 16b : Basidia up to 60 x 14 µm, mostly found under coniferous or birches.

17,CS34$ 17a : Pileus mostly red or orange, context slightly yellowing or unchanging (strongly yellowing only in R. arpalices). $ Subsect. Rhodellinae. $s"Subsect. Rhodellinae."
17,CS33$ 17b : Pileus variably coloured, context somewhat yellowing, at times with a scent of fruit or Pelargonium, stipe usually white. $ Subsect. Puellarinae. $s"Subsect. Puellarinae."

18,CS36$ 18a : Spore print cream or ochre, stipe often pinkish, found mostly under birches. $ Subsect. Betulinae. $s"Subsect. Betulinae."
18,CS35$ 18b : Spore print yellow, context sometimes becoming grey or slightly grey-brown (strongly yellowing only in R. sapinea), usually found under coniferous trees. $ Subsect. Laricinae. $s"Subsect. Laricinae."

19,20$ 19a : Dermatocystidia without incrustations.
19,CS24$ 19b : Epicutis with incrusted dermatocystidia, taste very acrid, context usually not turning blue with Gayac. $ Subsect. Rubrinae. $s"Subsect. Rubrinae."

20,0$ 20a : Pileus grey to olive brown (colours like series Pectinata), margin fairly sharp-edged and smooth, spore print cream, context greying, also slightly reddening, turning pink with Formalin. - Subsect. Consobrinae. $ R. consobrina. $s"Subsect. Consobrinae."
20,21$ 20b : Colours bright, red, violet, green, etc., combinations of characters different from the former (20a).

21,CS10$ 21a : Spore print white, possibly cream in some species with somewhat yellowish pileus, no scent of Pelargonium. $ Subsect. Russula. $s"Subsect. Russula."
21,CS16$ 21b : Spore print cream, scent more or less strong like Pelargonium, stipe never pink, context greying or slightly yellowing. $ Subsect. Violaceinae. $s"Subsect. Violaceinae."
21,CS15$ 21c : Context somewhat stiff, spore print cream or ochre (white only in R. luteotacta), stipe often pinkish or lilac, lamellae mostly adnate or subdecurrent, sometimes weeping, found under coniferous or birches (but some species with red pilei grow in deciduous forests). $ Subsect. Sardoninae. $s"Subsect. Sardoninae."
21,CS17$ 21d : Spore print yellow, rarely ochre (in this case stipe never pink), lamellae neither decurrent nor weeping. $ Subsect. Urentes. $s"Subsect. Urentes."

22,23$ 22a : Epicutis lacking primordial hyphae, dermatocystidia with or without incrustations (but Auratinae have dermatocystidia only in the cortex of stipe). $ Sect. Polychromae. $s"Sect. Polychromae."
22,25$ 22b : Epicutis containing both incrusted hyphae and dermatocystidia. $ Sect. Paraincrustatae. $s"Sect. Paraincrustatae."

23,0$ 23a : Dermatocystidia absent in epicutis (the latter consisting only of hyphal ends), but present in stipe cortex, context never turning purple with Phenol. – Subsect. Auratinae. $ R. aurea. $s"Subsect. Auratinae."
23,24$ 23b : Epicutis with dermatocystidia not incrusted.
23,CS30$ 23c : Epicutis with incrusted dermatocystidia, spore print yellow or ochre. $ Subsect. Paraintegrinae. $s"Subsect. Paraintegrinae."

24,CS26$ 24a : Context browning with age and greening with FeSO4, lamellae reddening with Aniline, smell tardily of herrings. $ Subsect. Xerampelinae. $s"Subsect. Xerampelinae."
24,CS27$ 24b : Context yellowing with age, but FeSO4 and Aniline reacting differently from the preceding Xerampelinae (24a), dried fruit bodies smelling often of honey, never of herrings, spores rounded and finely decorated, dermatocystidia with many septa and an emulsion-like content. $ Subsect. Melliolentinae. $s"Subsect. Melliolentinae"
24,CS28$ 24c : Dermatocystidia not or poorly septate (except in species with strongly yellow-browning context and echinulate spores), spore print yellow, ochre, rarely cream, context somewhat browning, rarely blackening, smell neither of herrings nor of honey when drying, reacting differently from Xerampelinae (24a) with FeSO4 and Aniline. $ Subsect. Integriforminae. $s"Subsect. Integriforminae."

25,CS31$ 25a : Spore print yellow (but cream-ochre in R. tyrrhenica), context moderately browning or blackening. $ Subsect. Integrae. $s"Subsect. Integrae."
25,CS32$ 25b : Spore print pale cream, context hard, taste bitter or somewhat minty in lamellae, cystidia of pileus and hymenium not blackening with SBA. $ Subsect. Lepidinae. $s"Subsect. Lepidinae."


Subgenus Compactae (Fr.) Bon

cs1,0$ Section Compactae Fr.

1,0$ 1a : Hymenial cystidia not turning black with SBA, pileocystidia comparatively large, never forked at the apex, enclosing oil drops not blackening with SBA, taste of menthol in lamellae, spores showing a very thin network with warts not exceeding 0.2 µm, context quickly blackening (first strongly reddening then blackening in fo. pseudonigricans). $ R. albonigra.
1,2$ 1b : Not with the above combination of characters.

2,3$ 2a : Epicutis lacking evident dermatocystidia, lamellae crowded, pileus dry or velutinous, context never reddening when cut. $ Series Atramentosa. $s"Series Atramentosa."
2,5$ 2b : Epicutis with dermatocystidia, but poorly characterized, very scattered and at times easily overlooked.

3,0$ 3a : Lamellae having a slightly refreshing taste, never acrid, spores 7-9 x 5.4-7 µm, cortex of stipe lacking oleiferous hyphae SBA+, found under deciduous and evergreen oaks (cf. R. anthracina var. insipida Romagn. nom. inval., with insipid lamellae and spores 8-10 x 6.5-8 µm). $ R. atramentosa.
3,4$ 3b : Taste distinctly acrid at least in lamellae, lamellar sinus showing a more or less slight pinkish tinge, oleiferous hyphae SBA+ occurring in stipe cortex.

4,0$ 4a : Spores 8-9.7 x 6.7-8 µm. $ R. anthracina var. anthracina.
4,0$ 4b : Spores 6.7-8.5 x 5.5-7 µm, lamellar sinus having a well distinct pink tinge. $ R. anthracina var. carneifolia.

5,0$ 5a : Fruit bodies sturdy and fleshy, lamellae thick and very distant (much less so in fo. heterophylla ad inter.) with a sinus shaded pale ochre, context strongly reddening before blackening (reddening weak or absent, later slowly blackening in fo. subadusta ad inter.), hyphal ends cylindric, 3-7 cm wide (more irregularly shaped, with inflated cells up to 12(18) µm in fo. megatricha ad inter.). $ R. nigricans.
5,0$ 5b : Found mostly under coniferous trees on sandy soil, also under broadleafed trees according to some Authors, fruit bodies sturdy and fleshy, lamellae distant with age, taste mild, context turning slowly very pale pink, then grey, spores finely reticulate, almost smooth. $ R. adusta.
5,6$ 5c : Not with the above combination of characters.

6,0$ 6a : Taste very acrid at least in lamellae, pileus 60-150 mm broad, subviscid, mostly turning reddish brown, context reddening before blackening when cut (soon blackening and with a blackish grey pileus in fo. pseudofuliginosa ad inter.), hyphal ends cylindric 3-4.5 µm wide [sinuous and 7-12(15) µm wide, with dermatocystidia 5-7 µm wide and pileus on average with more ochre-brown tinges in var. heteropellis ad inter.]. $ R. acrifolia.
6,7$ 6b : Taste mild to faintly acrid, pileus differently coloured, lamellae always crowded.

7,8$ 7a : Context not turning red, lamellae white.
7,0$ 7b : Context initially reddening, lamellae with a cream tinge, taste mild to slightly acrid, pileus 30-70 mm broad, mostly dry, milk and coffee coloured, sometimes with a slight olivaceous tinge, hyphal ends 6-12 µm wide. $ R. densifolia.

8,0$ 8a : Terminal elements of pileus hyphae cylindrical, spores comparatively oblong, 7.2-8.8 x 5.6-7 µm, pileus 50-100 mm broad. $ R. fuliginosa.
8,0$ 8b : Terminal elements of pileus hyphae subulate, spores subglobose 6.5-7.2(7.7) x 7.2-6.5(7) µm, pileus 40-65 mm broad, context sometimes hardly reddening. $ R. densissima.


cs2,0$ Section Archaeinae Heim ex Buyck & Sarn.

1,0$ 1a : Small species growing in central Africa, pileus not too fleshy, with a translucid crenulate margin (cf. also R. parvulospora Buyck). $ R. archaea.
1,2$ 1b : Fruit bodies fleshy, without translucid margin, found in Europe.

2,0$ 2a : Found in fresh mixed woods of subalpine and transalpine zones, feature Hygrophorus-like, due to the subdecurrent very thick and distant lamellae, context turning red with FeSO4, subcutis showing frequent inflated cells sometimes reaching the surface, hyphal ends often clavate, up to 6-8 µm wide or even wider. $ R. camarophylla.
2,0$ 2b : Found under Quercus suber in the Mediterranean area, lamellae moderately distant, context turning light pinkish orange with FeSO4, subcutis and cutis lacking inflated cells, hyphal ends very thin, 2-4 µm wide, spores slightly bigger, ornamentation more prominent than in the preceding species (2a) $ R. archaeosuberis.


cs3,0$ Section Lactarioides (Bat.) Konr. & Joss.

1,0$ 1a : Subcutis embedded in a gelatinous matrix having bluish shades, sporal ornamentation with long ridges forming a network like in R. romellii (cf. also R. gelatinascens Singer). $ R. chloroides var. glutinosa.
1,2$ 1b : Not with the above combination of characters.

2,3$ 2a : Spore print whitish cream (Ib-IIa).
2,5$ 2b : Spore print dark cream (IIc-d).
2,0$ 2c : Spore print yellow, taste burning. $ R. flavispora.

3,0$ 3a : Pileus irregular, with a flat-bottomed depression, stipe very short, lamellae comparatively broad and distant, sporal ornamentation consisting of a partial network with ridges and clustered warts, mostly on calcareous soil. $ R. delica var. delica.
3,0$ 3b : Lamellae comparatively broad and distant, but pileus regular and with more or less narrow funnel-shaped depression, spores like in R. chloroides. $ R. chloroides var. trachyspora.
3,4$ 3c : Pileus crater-like at last, lamellae narrower and more crowded.

4,0$ 4a : Stipe tending to be taller, spores 8-11.2 x 7.2-8.8 µm (var. parvispora has spores about 6.4-8 x 6-7 µm and context sometimes slightly reddening with FeSO4). $ R. chloroides var. chloroides.
4,0$ 4b : Pileus pure white at the beginning, smooth and slightly silky, margin thin, spores 7.5-9 x 6.5-7 µm, showing an entire network of thin lines, found on calcareous soil (not strictly ?). $ R. delica var. puta.

5,0$ 5a : Taste mild, then bitterish and astringent, odour of fruit, cystidia very slowly greying with SV (10-20'), sporal ornamentation consisting of small warts, at least partially reticulated. $ R. pallidospora.
5,0$ 5b : Taste moderately acrid, odour raphanoid, cystidia quickly blackening with SV, found especially under Pinus. $ R. littoralis.
5,0$ 5c : Spores sparsely aculeate, taste moderately acrid (northern species insufficiently confirmed). $ R. pseudodelica.


Subgenus Heterophyllidia Romagn., emend.

Section Heterophyllae Fr.

cs4,0$ Subsection Cyanoxanthinae Sing.

1,0$ 1a : Pileus cuticle areolate-cracked at least at the margin, the remaining characters like in 3c (weathered collections due to persistent rains, more frequent on clayey soil). $ R. cyanoxantha fo. cutefracta.
1,2$ 1b : Pileus cuticle never cracked.

2,0$ 2a : Taste more or less acrid, odour unpleasant but fugacious (character not well fixed). $ R. cyanoxantha var. variata.
2,3$ 2b : Taste mild, odour and taste not unpleasant.

3,0$ 3a : Pileus lemon yellow to green yellow ; spores "6.5-7(8.5) x 5.7-6.5(6.7) µm" (fide Romagnesi). $ R. cyanoxantha var. flavoviridis.
3,0$ 3b : Pileus from the beginning olive green. $ R. cyanoxantha fo. peltereaui.
3,0$ 3c : Pileus violaceous or with green patches, context turning slowly green with FeSO4. $ R. cyanoxantha var. cyanoxantha.
3,0$ 3d : Stipe mostly with a tinge of lilac, lamellae cream, context quickly green with FeSO4, epicutis occasionally with inflated cells (probably a form of R. cyanoxantha). $ R. langei.


cs5,0$ Subsection Heterophyllae (Fr.) J.Schaeff.

1,0$ 1a : Spore print cream, pileus honey brown or tawny brown to dark brown, epicutis lacking subulate hair-cells, found in mountain coniferous forests, very rarely under deciduous trees (Fagus). $ R. mustelina.
1,2$ 1b : Spores print white, epicutis with rigid, subulate, thick-walled hair-cells near the center of the pileus.

2,0$ 2a : Spores not exceeding 7 x 5.6 µm, pileus greenish (fo. heterophylla), lemon yellow (var. chlora ss. Romagn.), brownish (fo. adusta), partially orange (fo. laeticolor), wholly ivory cream (fo. virginea), lamellae forked and anastomosing at stipe attachment. $ R. heterophylla.
2,0$ 2b : Spores up to 8 x 6 µm, pileus lilac to violaceous brown, rarely light green (fo. viridata) or whitish (fo. lactea ss. auct., non ss. Fries). $ R. vesca.


cs6,0$ Subsection Griseinae J.Schaeff.

1,0$ 1a : Habitus Tenellae-like, spore print IIa-c. $ "Griseoflaccidae" ad inter., extra-european species. $s"Griseoflaccidae."
1,2$ 1b : Normally facies and consistency Griseinae-like.

2,0$ 2a : Spore print light yellow, IIIc to IVa-b, spores large, reticulate measuring 7.6-9.6 x 6.4-8 µm, big synantropic species having a mild taste (if spores almost smooth cf. R. werneri). $ R. ochrospora.
2,3$ 2b : Spore print pure white (white-spored Griseinae) (cf. R. vesca and R. heterophylla, having poorly characterized dermatocystidia and context orange-reddish with FeSO4).
2,4$ 2c : Spore print cream, exceptionally ochre.

3,0$ 3a : Colours of pileus and spores like in R. vesca, taste acrid, dermatocystidia with septa, lower cells of hyphal ends subisodiametric (species not confirmed). $ R. johannis.
3,0$ 3b : Taste entirely mild, pileus with violet and green colours, mostly under Picea (cf. also R. leucospora, possibly conspecific). $ R. variegatula.

4,0$ 4a : Sporal ornamentation with tiny warts 0.1-0.25 µm high, hyphal ends 3-6 µm wide, pileus variegated with violet, green, brown colours, stipe clavate (when stipe shortly obconic, sometimes with an olive grey tinge, odour weak of fruit and resin, taste a little refreshing and astringent in lamellae, cf. var. subostreata ad inter.). $ R. sublevispora.
4,0$ 4b : Found under mountain conifers on sandy soil, occasionally also under deciduous trees (Fagus), pileus honey-brown to fulvous brown, fruit bodies thick-set, context bright orange with FeSO4, spores large, variably crested, hyphal ends 3-7 µm wide, dermatocystidia poorly characterized (in subsect. Heterophyllae). $ Cf. R. mustelina.
4,5$ 4c : Not with the above combination of characters.

5,0$ 5a : Context not turning pink with FeSO4, hyphal ends cylindrical, 2-3-4 µm wide, spores at least partially reticulate. $ (cf. R. helgae, R. werneri, R. ilicis in subsect. Ilicinae).
5,6$ 5b : Context turning pink with FeSO4, pileus pure green, hyphal ends with few septa, only 3-4(6) µm wide, spore print IIb-c (if spore print IIIa-b, cf. R. medullata).
5,0$ 5c : Spore print pale cream (IIa), hyphal ends thick, made up of short, inflated cells, 5-10 µm wide, mostly found under Fagus. $ R. ionochlora.
5,7$ 5d : Not with the above combination of characters.

6,0$ 6a : Dermatocystidia cylindric, 4-7 µm wide, partially capitate, spores oblong more or less cristulate, found under birches or in coniferous woods (cf. subsect. Ilicinae). $ Cf. R. aeruginea.
6,0$ 6b : Dermatocystidia clavate, 6-9 µm wide (never capitate according to Romagnesi), spores cristulate to locally subreticulate (cf. green forms of R. grisea), very rare species found under deciduous trees). $ R. stenotricha.

7,0$ 7a : Found under birches, spores entirely reticulate and with tiny warts, dermatocystidia septate. $ R. betulae.
7,8$ 7b : Hyphal ends (5)6-9(12) µm wide, spore print cream, II(b)c-d (cf. R. ionochlora with spore print IIa), FeSO4 reaction medium to almost negative, never strong.
7,14$ 7c : With different characters, hyphal ends only 4-6 µm wide.

8,9$ 8a : Sporal ornamentation showing isolated warts, found under Cistus or in deciduous woods, mostly oaks.
8,10$ 8b : Spores with some ridges sometimes forming a partial network, context turning pink with FeSO4 (in case of next to negative reaction cf. R. atroglauca).

9,0$ 9a : Found under Cistus monspeliensis, pileus green, 20-50(70) mm broad, usually neither scurfy nor cracked, hyphal ends 4-7(9) µm wide, made up of relatively long cells. $ R. monspeliensis var. monspeliensis.
9,0$ 9b : Found under Cistus shrubs bordering Quercus suber or Q. ilex woods on siliceous soil, pileus 40-100 mm broad, scurfy and cracked like in R. anatina, hyphal ends thicker, about 6-12 µm wide. $ R. monspeliensis var. sejuncta.
9,0$ 9c : Found under deciduous oaks, rarely Tilia, Fagus, Betula, pileus more or less scurfy and cracked, grey to blackish green (smoother and lilac in fo. subvesca), FeSO4 slowly greenish grey, spores smaller, with isolated warts, hyphal ends 5-10 µm wide. $ R. anatina.

10,0$ 10a : Spores 5.2-7 x 4.7-5.7 µm, with an ornamentation of short ridges, basidia 6.5-9 µm wide, lamellae sometimes forked and decurrent, pileus more or less violet, bluish, greenish, often very pale. $ R. subterfurcata.
10,11$ 10b : Spores and basidia larger.

11,13$ 11a : Pileus colours very pale on an ochraceous ivory background, context pink with FeSO4 (cf. R. galochroides with narrower hyphal ends).
11,12$ 11b : Pileus olive green, rarely slightly bluish.

12,0$ 12a : FeSO4 pink, pileus green (bluish in fo. caerulea ad inter.), spores subreticulate, hyphal ends having the lower cells mostly subisodiametric and the apical ones often elongate, found under deciduous trees, mainly under Quercus on calcareous soil. $ R. pseudoaeruginea.
12,0$ 12b : Found under birches, pileus olive to dark bluish green, taste mild, context not turning pink with FeSO4, sporal ornamentation having thin more or less branched ridges, hyphal ends huge but not distinctive (lower cells are not so different from the apical ones). $ R. atroglauca.

13,0$ 13a : Pileus 30-60 mm broad, with a pale tinge of lilac or greenish-grey on a whitish background, spores with prominent almost isolated warts, hyphal ends consisting of subisodiametric cells, found under birches, oaks, etc. $ R. galochroa.
13,0$ 13b : Pileus 40-100(120) mm broad, very pale, with ochre to hazel grey or rarely olive grey patches, sporal ridges sometimes fusing into a partial network, found under Fagus (cf. R. subterfurcata). $ R. faustiana.
13,0$ 13c : Pileus ivory cream or with a shade of greyish green (the remaining characters like in 12a). $ R. pseudoaeruginea fo. galochroa.

14,0$ 14a : Context lilac under the cuticle and in nibbled parts, possibly also along the distal edge of lamellae in wet weather, stipe mostly with lilac shades (also green in fo. pictipes), pileus violet, bluish, or partially green, FeSO4 bright orange, spore print IIc. $ R. grisea.
14,15$ 14b : FeSO4 reaction not distinctive (pale orange pink).

15,0$ 15a : Spores having isolated warts, spore print ochre (IIIa-b), hyphal ends 3-4-5 µm wide, taste mostly entirely mild. $ R. medullata.
15,16$ 15b : Sporal ornamentation with ridges forming a partial network, spore print IIa-IIb.

16,0$ 16a : Pileus ivory cream with a shade of grey or greenish, fruit bodies small, found in Quercus ilex undergrowth with Erica arborea. $ R. galochroides.
16,0$ 16b : Fruit bodies medium-sized, pileus dark green to blackish blue, partially violaceous, widespread in deciduous or coniferous woods. $ R. parazurea.


cs7,0$ Subsection Ilicinae (Romagn.) Buyck

1,0$ 1a : Pileus dark olive to dark purple brown resembling R. sororia, spores small, about 6-7 x 4.5-5.5 µm, spore print IIb-c. $ R. helgae.
1,2$ 1b : Not with the above combination of characters, pileus sometimes very pale, with Heterophyllidia-like colours.

2,3$ 2a : Found under evergreen oaks, fruit bodies fleshy and mostly sturdy, very pale-coloured, context hardly reacting with FeSO4, hyphal ends cylindric, with few septa, 2-3(4) µm wide, spores at least with a partial network.
2,4$ 2b : Pileus distinctly green (but pale or whitish variants are likely to be met with), context turning pink with FeSO4, hyphal ends cylindric, not wider than 4-5(6) µm, spore print IIb-c (if spore print IIIa-b, cf. stenotrichoid forms of R. medullata).

3,0$ 3a : Spore print IIIc-IVa, sporal ornamentation consisting of a very thin network hardly visible under O. M., found in sunny sites with siliceous soil under Quercus suber. $ R. werneri.
3,0$ 3b : Spore print about IId, sporal network ornamentation easily visible under O. M., fruit bodies sturdy and fleshy, found under Quercus ilex, very rarely under Q. pubescens. $ R. ilicis.

4,0$ 4a : Dermatocystidia cylindric, 4-7 µm wide, mostly capitate, spores oblong, showing thin ridges, found under birch or conifers. $ R. aeruginea.
4,0$ 4b : Dermatocystidia clavate, hardly capitate, 6-9 µm wide, sporal ornamentation at least locally consisting of a partial reticulum (cf. green forms of R. grisea). $ R. stenotricha.


cs8,0$ Subgenus Amoenula Sarn.

1,2$ 1a : Empty pleurocystidia present and very conspicuous, about 10-20 µm wide, context turning light brown with Phenol.
1,0$ 1b : Empty pleurocystidia none, lower cells of hyphal ends globose, context turning violet with Phenol. $ R. amoena var. acystidiata.
1,3$ 1c : Empty pleurocystidia not wider than 10 µm.

2,0$ 2a : Spore print light cream (IIa-IIb), lower cells of hyphal cells mostly globose, pileus initially flattened, purplish violet, possibly white green, yellow or brown patches, stipe usually carmine, found in fresh deciduous or coniferous woods. $ R. violeipes.
2,0,N$ Pileus lemon yellow, stipe whitish, other characters like in the preceding one (fo. violeipes)  $ R. violeipes fo. citrina.
2,0$ 2b : Spore print dark cream (IIc) IId, lower cells of hyphal ends rarely globose, up to 8(10) µm wide, mostly under thermophilous or evergreen trees (Quercus, etc.), pileus purple, sometimes green or brown, or variegated (fo. amoenicolor), wholly green (fo. olivacea), dark purple with blackish center (fo. nigrosanguinea, nom. inval.). $ R. amoenicolor.

3,0$ 3a : Context turning violet with Phenol, lower cells of hyphal ends not wider than 11(14) µm, pileus 20-50(60) mm, violaceous purple (fo. amoena), olive green (fo. viridis), etc. $ R. amoena.
3,0$ 3b : Context turning brownish with Phenol, lower cells of hyphal ends globose, 12-18-24 µm wide, spore print IId to IIIa, pileus 40-120 mm broad. $ R. amoenicolor var. stenocystidiata.


cs9,0$ Subgenus Ingratula Romagn.

1,0$ 1a : Remnants of a yellowish veil at stipe base turning immediately red with KOH, basal mycelium yellow, taste mild. – Sect. Subvelatae Sing. $ R. insignis. $s"Sect. Subvelatae."
1,2$ 1b : Yellow veil lacking, KOH not turning the stipe base red.

2,3$ 2a : Spore print pure white, large dermatocystidia in epicutis, odour pleasant of fruit or Pelargonium.
2,5$ 2b : Spore print cream, exceptionally ochre (?), dermatocystidia small to medium-sized. $ Subsect. Foetentinae (Melz. & Zv.) Sing. $s"Subsect. Foetentinae."

3,0$ 3a : Both pileal margin and lamellar distal end obtuse, pileus honey-ochre to tawny-ochre, stipe slowly becoming concolorous, spores having an amyloid spot, dermatocystidia cylindrical well characterized, pigment incrusting. – Sect. Felleinae (Melz. & Zv.) Sarn., emend. $ R. fellea. $s"Sect. Felleinae."
3,4$ 3b : Pileal margin acute, strongly tuberculate-striate, dermatocystidia mostly large and spindle-shaped, spores lacking an amyloid spot. $ Subsect. Farinipedes Sing. $s"Subsect. Farinipedes."

4,0$ 4a : Found in deciduous woods, context subelastic, pileus ochre-yellow somewhat uniform, spores 6.4-8 x 5.8-6.7 µm, with thin scattered spines, odour fruity, Gayac hardly reacting on context. $ R. farinipes.
4,0$ 4b : Very rare northern species found in coniferous woods, pileus sometimes showing a flattened umbo, spores subglobose, 7.2-9 x 6.4-7.4 µm, ornamentation consisting of low warts and some ridges. $ R. pallescens.

5,6$ 5a : Pileus tawny-ochre, lamellae often weeping, odours generally strong. $ Series Foetens. $s"Series Foetens."
5,11$ 5b : Small to exceptionally medium-sized fruit bodies, pileus brownish ochre to smoky-brown, to ashy-grey, pileus margin strongly tuberculate-striate, dermatocystidia tiny and lanceolate. $ Series Pectinata. $s"Series Pectinata."

6,7$ 6a : Scent of bitter almond, anise, or marzipan, spores somewhat globose. $ Subseries Laurocerasi. $s"Subseries Laurocerasi."
6,8$ 6b : Odour unpleasant, sweetish-oily or hypochlorite-like, occasionally also fruity. $ Subseries Foetens. $s"Subseries Foetens."

7,0$ 7a : Odour anise-like strong and persistent, lasting a few weeks in dried specimens, pileus margin smooth or shortly striate. $ R. fragrantissima.
7,0$ 7b : Edge of lamellae and stipe with blackish brown dots, pileus glutinous in young fruit bodies, odour a mixture of bitter almonds and R. foetens, taste of context acrid in stipe. $ R. illota.
7,0$ 7c : Edge of lamellae and stipe lacking dark dots, pileus never glutinous, scent pure of bitter almonds, taste mild in the stipe, sporal ornamentation consisting of huge winged ridges. $ R. laurocerasi.

8,9$ 8a : Context never with lemon yellow shades when cut, not turning yellow with KOH, spores mostly large and often globose. $ Stirps Foetens. $s"Stirps Foetens."
8,10$ 8b : Context slowly turning lemon yellow when cut, at least in young fruit bodies, often turning yellow with KOH.

9,0$ 9a : Big-sized fruit bodies, pileus glutinous when young and in wet weather, margin acute, soon strongly tuberculate-sulcate, odour unpleasant, taste very acrid, spores with projecting somewhat isolated spines. $ R. foetens.
9,0$ 9b : Medium-sized fruit bodies, pileus lacking gluten, spores 7.5-9.8 x 6.5-8.4 µm, ornamentation with ridges forming a partial network, growing mostly in coastal pinewoods with Cistus in the Mediterranean zone. $ R. putida.

10,0$ 10a : Spores oblong, 7.2-9.6 x 6-7.2 µm, with low somewhat scattered warts, context turning yellow with KOH. $ R. subfoetens.
10,0$ 10b : Spores like in R. subfoetens, context inconstantly yellowing with KOH, smell of fresh butter in young specimen, then unpleasant, found under Pinus or Quercus (probably the same as the former 10a). $ R. josserandii.
10,0$ 10c : Spores subglobose not longer than 8.5 µm, with spines partially joined by ridges, context not yellowing with KOH, found in Mediterranean area, mostly under Quercus. $ R. inamoena.

11,0$ 11a : Odour of Pelargonium, spore print ochre (IIIa-b). $ Cf. R. foeda.
11,12$ 11b : Odour different, spore print cream.

12,13$ 12a : Taste acrid.
12,14$ 12b : Taste mild or sometimes bitterish, odour unpleasant, rubbery or only fishy.

13,0$ 13a : Fruit bodies thickset, pileus ochre like in R. farinipes, sometimes with smoky-brown or rusty-brown spots, occasionally with purplish red stains at stipe base, odour like in R. foetens. $ R. pectinata.
13,0$ 13b : Fruit bodies mostly medium-sized, pileus dark smoky-brown to ashy-grey, sometimes spotted rusty-brown, odour spermatic, context usually faintly turning blue with Gayac, spores sometimes subglobose. $ R. sororia.
13,0$ 13c : Small-sized fruit bodies, pileus dark like in the preceding species, odour like in Lactarius volemus, then subspermatic, spores elongate, context turning blue with Gayac. $ R. amoenolens.

14,0$ 14a : Widespread under Quercus or coniferous-trees in mediterranean area, taste unpleasant and sometimes bitterish, odour rubbery or fishy, pileus mostly ochre-brown, rusty-brown spotted as the remaining parts of the fruit body, often with purplish red spots at the stipe base near the basal mycelium, sporal ornamentation showing a partial reticulum (if the spores have warts isolated or faintly crestate, cf. R. pectinatoides ss. Romagn., non Peck). $ R. praetervisa.
14,0$ 14b : Taste not unpleasant, odour faintly fishy, then strong of salted anchovy or of stockfish on drying, pileus grey to grey-brown, rusty-brown spots rare, tardy and faint, base of stipe lacking purplish red stains, spores reticulate like in R. violeipes, found in gardens or parks under Quercus ilex. $ R. hortensis.


Subgenus Russula, emend.

Section Russula, emend.

cs10,0$ Subsection Russula, emend.

1,3$ 1a : Pileus red, sometimes pinkish, rarely with a shade of carmine, possibly faded, spore print white. $ Series Russula. $s"Series Russula."
1,2$ 1b :Pileus purple, violet, partially or entirely yellow, green, brown, very rarely red (due to pigmental dissociation), spore print white or cream. $ Series Atropurpurea. $s"Series Atropurpurea."

2,CS11$ 2a : Pileus purple to violet, possibly yellow or green at disc, sometimes blackish, exceptionally brownish, spore print usually white. $ Stirp Atropurpurea. $s"Stirp Atropurpurea."
2,CS12$ 2b : Pileus yellow, yellow green, rarely with orange patches, spore print white or cream (cf. also yellow or green forms of stirp Atropurpurea). $ Stirp Citrina. $s"Stirp Citrina."

3,CS13$ 3a : Spores large, sporal ornamentation aculeate, spines high and at least partly connected by thin lines so as to form a partial network, context hardly turning blue with Gayac. $ Stirp Emetica. $s"Stirp Emetica."
3,CS14$ 3b : Sporal ornamentation finely warty, often densely reticulate, context sometimes turning blue with Gayac. $ Stirp Mairei. $s"Stirp Mairei."
3,0$ 3c : Found under deciduous trees, context rigid, often staining chrome yellow in broken parts, lamellae mostly decurrent, very distant in typical forms, dewy in wet weather (in subsect. Sardoninae). $ Cf. R. luteotacta.


cs11,0$ Stirp Atropurpurea

1,2$ 1a : Species mostly found under alpine dwarf shrubs, at times associated with Salix in the riparial vegetation of northern Europe.
1,0$ 1b : Found in mountain shrubberies of Alnobetula viridis, as far as the lowlands associated with Alnus glutinosa or incana ("R. pumila"), stipe becoming grey or grey brown, sometimes at the beginning yellow all over, pileus 20-40(50) mm, taste mild or slightly acrid. $ R. alnetorum.
1,3$ 1c : Found in woods, or in association with other mycorrhizal hosts.

2,0$ 2a : Spores 7-9 x 5.6-7.2 µm, ornamentation consisting of thin lines forming a reticulum, dermatocystidia 6-9 µm wide, 0-1 septate, context slowly turning blue with Gayac, odour like coconut or fruit. $ R. laccata.
2,0$ 2b : Odour like amyl acetate (R. fragilis), context mostly hardly turning blue with Gayac [species not confirmed, looking like the former (2a) in the remaining characters]. $ R. alpigenes.

3,0$ 3a : Pileus 50-120 mm broad, variously coloured, context compact, strongly greying when wet, spores 7-9 x 5.7-7.2 µm. $ R. atropurpurea.
3,0$ 3b : Found under birches, rarely Picea, context fragile, pileus light pink to partially violaceous, mostly fading to white, taste very acrid, spores large, ornamentation consisting of spines and thin lines forming an incomplete network, edge of lamellae sometimes serrate. $ R. betularum.
3,4$ 3c : Not with the above combination of characters.

4,5$ 4a : Taste usually markedly acrid, odour like amyl acetate.
4,6$ 4b : Taste faintly or moderately acrid, sometimes mild.

5,0$ 5a : Spores subglobose, comparatively large, reticulate, edge of lamellae often serrulate, context not turning blue with Gayac, widespread on dry soil under deciduous or coniferous trees. $ R. fragilis.
5,0$ 5b : Spores small, oblong, commonly found in damp coniferous woods of the subalpine zone, as far up as northern Europe, context turning blue with Gayac. $ R. atrorubens.

6,7$ 6a : Found in dry deciduous woods.
6,0$ 6b : Found in moist coniferous forests with Sphagnum, pileus carmine red, often with a shade of brown, context very fragile and watery, stipe apically constricted. $ R. aquosa.

7,0$ 7a : Found under Quercus, mainly evergreen oaks, context faintly acrid, spores 6.6-8.8 x (5.2)5.5-7 µm, ornamentation crestate-reticulate, pileus purple to violet, to green, etc., very variable in colours (wholly yellow in fo. heliochroma). $ R. poikilochroa.
7,0$ 7b : Pileus bright red like in R. minutula, spores 6.7-7 x 6-6.2 µm, basidia and cystidia not wider than 10 µm. $ R. rubrocarminea.


cs12,0$ Stirp Citrina

1,2$ 1a : Spore print cream to light ochre, between IIa-IIIa (cf. yellow or green forms of subsect. Sardoninae).
1,3$ 1b : Spore print pure white (cf. yellow or green forms of stirp Atropurpurea, especially R. atropurpurea fo. dissidens, R. fragilis var. gilva, R. poikilochroa fo. heliochroma).

2,0$ 2a : Only found under Fagus, pileus small and fragile, chrome yellow or with a tawny orange patch at the disc, sometimes wholly of this colour, spore print IIc (IIIa), sporal ornamentation aculeate. $ R. solaris.
