3,0$ 3a : Pileus light lemon yellow (also green, according to some Authors), usually whitish near the margin, sometimes fading to white all over, rarely light green, spores finely reticulate, found in deciduous or coniferous woods. $ R. raoultii.
3,0$ 3b : Found on Sphagnum, context turning strongly grey, spores wider than in the preceding species, hyphal ends cylindric. $ R. pseudoraoultii.
3,0$ 3c : Pileus yellow, possibly with a tawny shade at disc, stipe surface and nibbled parts changing to rusty brown, spores 6.7-8.2 x 5.2-6.7 µm, warty with a partial network of thin lines. $ R. citrina.


cs13,0$ Stirp Emetica

1,0$ 1a : Found in moist sites under Betula, exceptionally under Picea, fruit bodies small and fragile, pileus pinkish to partially violaceous, fading, sometimes entirely white (in series Atropurpurea). $ Cf. R. betularum.
1,0$ 1b : Pileus up to 100(120) mm, spores about 8.8-10.5 x 7.4-8.8 µm, found in peat-bogs especially under coniferous trees. $ R. emetica.
1,0$ 1c : Pileus non larger than 60 mm, spores 7.2-9.8 x 6.2-7.8 µm, found in deciduous or coniferous woods on sandy soils, usually muscicolous (Leucobryum or Polytrichum). $ R. silvestris.


cs14,0$ Stirp Mairei

1,0$ 1a : Found along paths in thermophilous oak woods, mostly on clayey soils, pileus bright red to pinkish, sometimes with a tinge of carmine, context becoming slowly dirty grey due to dehydratation, also blackening on handling, odour of amyl acetate like in R. fragilis, spores 7.4-9.5 x (6)6.4-7.8 µm. $ R. rhodomelanea.
1,0$ 1b : Found under broadleafed trees, context never greying or blackening, smelling like in R. fragilis, lamellae purely white, spores 7-8.2 x 6-7 µm. $ R. emeticella.
1,2$ 1c : Not with the above combination of characters.

2,0$ 2a : Found under deciduous trees, mostly Fagus, context unchanging or tardily slightly yellowing, smelling like in R. emetica, lamellae crowded, white, often with a more or less distinct bluish shade in young fruit bodies. $ R. mairei.
2,3$ 2b : Context turning grey when wet, lamellae without a blue shade.

3,0$ 3a : Tiny alpine species, also found in mixed woods of northern lowlands, lamellae distant, spores subglobose, with a very thin ornamentation, dermatocystidia mostly cylindric, hyphal ends larger than in the following species. $ R. nana.
3,0$ 3b : Found on Sphagnum, but also in comparatively dry sites under deciduous and coniferous trees, lamellae often subdecurrent, sporal ornamentation higher than in R. nana, dermatocystidia more clavate. $ R. grisescens.


cs15,0$ Subsection Sardoninae Sing., emend.

1,2$ 1a : Pileus purple red to violaceous, sometimes partially yellow, green, brown, colours at times mixed or dissociated (some forms lacking red, blue, etc.), found under coniferous trees. $ Series Sardonia. $s"Series Sardonia."
1,8$ 1b : Also under conifers, but pileus pure red (cf. red forms of the preceding group). $ Series Sanguinea. $s"Series Sanguinea."
1,9$ 1c : Found under birches, pileus usually polychromatic, stipe turning distinctly grey when wet. $ Series Exalbicans. $s"Series Exalbicans."
1,11$ 1d : Found under deciduous trees (including birches), pileus pure red, spore print white or cream. $ Series Persicina. $s"Series Persicina."

